            <ArticleTitle>Self-propulsion of Leidenfrost Drops between Non-Parallel Structures.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>12018</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41598-017-12279-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this work, we explored self-propulsion of a Leidenfrost drop between non-parallel structures. A theoretical model was first developed to determine conditions for liquid drops to start moving away from the corner of two non-parallel plates. These conditions were then simplified for the case of a Leidenfrost drop. Furthermore, ejection speeds and travel distances of Leidenfrost drops were derived using a scaling law. Subsequently, the theoretical models were validated by experiments. Finally, three new devices have been developed to manipulate Leidenfrost drops in different ways.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
